<LabLayout title="Designer method · Tether Lab" description="Geometry, gravity and scope of the G1 asymmetric-tether coast model. Separate hardware position from the center of mass, and initial orbital elements from the propagated trajectory." active="designer">
<main id="lab-content" class="guide">
  <header class="guide-header"><p class="micro">G1-0.1.0 / GEOMETRY BEFORE OPERATIONS</p><h1>Design the machine. Observe its coast.</h1><p>A fixed-mass, planar rigid-body experiment for unequal arms and circular or elliptical initial orbits. This is a geometry foundation, not a complete transportation mission.</p><a class="button-link" href="/lab/designer/">Back to Designer →</a></header>
  <div class="guide-layout"><nav class="guide-nav" aria-label="On this page"><a href="#geometry">Hub and mass center</a><a href="#orbit">Initial orbit</a><a href="#loads">Loads and limits</a><a href="#numerics">Numerical checks</a><a href="#next">What comes next</a></nav><div class="guide-body">
    <section id="geometry" class="guide-section"><h2>The hub is hardware, not a prescribed balance point.</h2><p>Arm A extends in the negative direction from the physical hub; Arm B extends in the positive direction. Each has an independent length and terminal mass. The hub mass can also change. The cable has one selected material and hub cross-section. A tapered profile falls to 30% of that area at each tip, using that arm’s own length.</p><p>Mass and inertia are calculated from cable quadrature cells plus the hub and terminal masses. The numerical state locates the instantaneous center of mass, while each hardware point retains its distance from the hub. Moving the center of mass does not secretly resize an arm or place the hub there.</p><pre class="equation-panel" tabindex="0">s_center = Σ mᵢ sᵢ / M
qᵢ = sᵢ − s_center
I = Σ mᵢ qᵢ²
rᵢ = R + qᵢ [cos θ, sin θ]
vᵢ = V + ω qᵢ [−sin θ, cos θ]</pre><p>The live geometry diagram shows the draft. The orbit scene and result report show the last calculated design until a new run completes. Their labels explicitly distinguish those two states.</p></section>
    <section id="orbit" class="guide-section"><h2>An ellipse is an initial condition, not a rail.</h2><p>Perigee and apogee are altitudes above the model Earth radius. They define an initial Kepler ellipse for the center of mass. Equal values define a circle. Starting orbital angle is true anomaly measured from perigee; for a circle that direction is simply the chosen reference axis.</p><p>Tether angle is measured from the local outward radial direction toward End B. The selected spin period initializes prograde inertial rotation. Subsequent translation and spin follow the distributed gravity calculation, not an imposed circular orbit or fixed spin rate.</p><pre class="equation-panel" tabindex="0">a = (r_perigee + r_apogee) / 2
e = (r_apogee − r_perigee) / (r_apogee + r_perigee)
p = a (1 − e²)
r = p / (1 + e cos ν)
v = √(μ/p) [−sin ν, e + cos ν]</pre><p>The dashed ellipse is the initial point-mass reference. The thin amber line is the full calculated center-of-mass path. Distributed-body gravity can make those differ. Globe and orbital-plane views use the same calculation; the Earth lighting and enlarged markers are illustrative.</p></section>
    <section id="loads" class="guide-section"><h2>Read the axial result within its boundary.</h2><p>Gravity acts on the distributed mass cells, creating a summed force and a moment about the mass center. No engines, fuel flow or capture impulse are added. Axial force is reconstructed from the rigid-body acceleration and external gravity on one side of each section.</p><p>A real cable cannot carry bending like a rigid rod. This experiment checks axial tension, but does not resolve transverse deformation or the flexible dynamics that may invalidate the assumed straight configuration. Passing its checks therefore does not establish a realizable taut-cable trajectory.</p><p>For tapered profiles, each quadrature interval uses its smaller boundary area, so a heavy terminal is checked at the narrow tip rather than only at an interior midpoint. This conservative discrete-area convention differs from the older Flight Studio’s midpoint load sampling; it does not silently modify the older engine.</p><p>The calculation stops if any point along the straight cable crosses 120 km altitude, the axial stress exceeds ultimate fiber strength divided by the chosen factor, or a section requires compression beyond a 100 N numerical tolerance. Crossings detected at step endpoints are bracketed within that step. Neither exhaustive continuous-time bounds nor failure motion are claimed.</p><p>Material properties are the same declared fiber or hypothetical inputs described in the <a href="/lab/method/#materials">existing material guide</a>. Joints, fatigue, creep, radiation, debris, manufacturing defects and environmental strength losses are not calculated.</p></section>
    <section id="numerics" class="guide-section"><h2>Check the equations independently of the picture.</h2><p>G1 uses SI units, default 48 cable cells split between the arms with at least four per arm, and 2-second RK4 steps. Observation is specified in initial Kepler periods, with a maximum of two periods and a separate 24-hour cap. Both are input limits, not a validated operating envelope.</p><p>Tests compare uniform symmetric geometry against the existing dry single-stage model, asymmetric mass moments against analytic expressions, orbit initialization against its prescribed apses, force against the gradient of potential energy, and a non-radial elliptical trajectory against an independently coded SciPy DOP853 reference. Step and quadrature refinement, mirror symmetry, cutoff behavior, input validation and saved-design round trips have separate checks.</p><p>The drift values in the result are maxima at accepted integration steps, not interval-arithmetic guarantees. Replay reintegrates from the previous recorded sample without altering the original run. Small energy and angular-momentum errors test numerical consistency; they do not validate a physical cable.</p><details class="guide-details"><summary>Saved designs and reports</summary><div><p>G1 uses its own model identifier, browser-storage key and bounded JSON format. Existing Flight Studio and Operations files are rejected rather than silently reinterpreted. Export design stores inputs; Export coast report includes the accepted design, numerical settings, starting/final state and full-run extrema. Unrun edits cannot relabel an older report.</p></div></details></section>
    <section id="next" class="guide-section"><h2>Coast first. Recovery and two-tier dynamics next.</h2><p>No payload transfers, chemical or electrodynamic recovery, T4 pivot dynamics or Operations manifest are included in G1 yet. Elliptical recovery needs a suitable reference trajectory and controller; attaching the existing circular controller would not preserve the design intent.</p><p>The next architectural step is a genuinely coupled secondary rotor with pivot reactions and independent conservation tests. Its release and encounter logic must then be connected to a finite manifest. Those are future implementations, not options hidden behind this interface.</p><a class="button-link" href="/lab/designer/">Return to your geometry →</a></section>
  </div></div>
